## Ramadan in brief

Ramadan is the ninth month of the Islamic calendar, which is a lunar calendar. Its dates therefore move forward by about eleven days each year compared with the Gregorian calendar: check them for the year of your trip.

During this month, Muslims fast from dawn to sunset. The fast is broken every evening with a meal called **ftour** in Morocco. The month ends with a major celebration, **Eid al-Fitr**.

## The rhythm of the day

The country lives at a different pace:

- during the day, things are calmer, especially in the afternoon;
- opening hours of offices, shops and some sights may be shorter or shifted;
- just before sunset, the streets empty: it is time for the ftour with the family;
- in the evening, the towns come alive again, sometimes until late at night.

{% tip title="Planning your visits" %}
Do your sightseeing in the morning and keep the evenings for wandering the medinas, which take on a special atmosphere during this month.
{% /tip %}

## Eating and drinking

Non-Muslim travellers are not expected to fast. In the main tourist cities, many hotels, riads and restaurants continue to serve food during the day. In smaller towns and less touristy neighbourhoods, many cafés and small eateries close until the evening.

The ftour is a highlight. It often includes **harira**, a soup of tomatoes, lentils and chickpeas, dates, hard-boiled eggs, honey pastries such as **chebakia**, as well as milk and coffee. Some restaurants and riads offer a ftour to travellers: it is a great way to discover it.

## Good habits

- Out of respect, avoid eating, drinking or smoking openly in the street during the day. Keep a bottle of water in your bag and drink discreetly.
- Be patient: fatigue and heat can make late afternoons more tense, especially on the road.
- Avoid driving just before sunset, when traffic can be in a hurry.
- Wear clothes that cover your shoulders and knees, as you would all year round in traditional places.

## Eid al-Fitr

Eid al-Fitr marks the end of Ramadan. It is a family celebration: many Moroccans travel to be with their relatives. In the days around it, trains, coaches and shared taxis are in high demand, and some shops close.

{% warning title="Transport around Eid" %}
If your trip coincides with Eid, book your train or coach tickets in advance and leave some slack in your plans.
{% /warning %}

For more on getting around, read [Getting around Morocco](/en/guides/getting-around-morocco).
